# Environment Configuration Template
# This file is auto-generated by setup.sh
# Edit values below to customize your setup

# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Python Configuration
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1
PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1
PYTHONHASHSEED=0

# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# LLM Backend Selection (see app/llm_backend.py)
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# auto = MLX on Apple Silicon (Darwin+arm64), Ollama everywhere else.
# Force a backend with: LLM_PROVIDER=ollama or LLM_PROVIDER=mlx
# NOTE for Docker: containers run Linux, so leave this unset/auto —
# setting LLM_PROVIDER=mlx inside containers would look for a local
# MLX server that only exists on the Mac host.
# Sized by scripts/predeploy.py — see the Machine Profile block at the bottom.

# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Ollama LLM Server Configuration (Windows / Linux)
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# URL to Ollama server (default: local).
# Use the literal IPv4, NOT "localhost". On Windows "localhost" resolves to
# ::1 first while Ollama binds IPv4 only, so every model-resolution call pays
# ~2,066 ms in SYN retransmits before falling back to 127.0.0.1. Measured on
# this box: localhost 2,034 ms vs 127.0.0.1 5 ms. Docker overrides this with
# http://ollama:11434, so changing it here does not affect containers.

# Keep the model resident between calls. -1 = never unload. Must be an INT
# (Ollama's Go duration parser rejects the string "-1" with a 400); a duration
# such as "24h" stays a string. Without this the first chat resets the boot
# pre-warm to Ollama's 5-minute default and the next caller pays a cold reload.

# Context window for small utility calls (intent detection, lead extraction,
# sentiment). LEAVE UNSET unless you have measured a reason to differ.
# Ollama keeps one runner per model and tears it down whenever a request
# arrives with a different num_ctx, so five call sites with their own values
# (512/1024/2048/4096) against the voice path's 8192 caused a ~6-11 s reload
# on the next voice turn. The code now defaults every call site to
# OLLAMA_NUM_CTX, so agreement holds without this key. Set it only to
# deliberately diverge -- and expect the reload cost if you do.
# SMALL_TASK_NUM_CTX=8192

# Model to use and context window size — sized per machine by
# scripts/predeploy.py (see the Machine Profile block at the bottom).
# Q3_K_M chosen for 6 GB VRAM cards; 14B/7B-4bit MLX on Apple Silicon.
# Voice calls run the production voice system prompt (~3.5k tokens) plus
# RAG context, so the context window must stay ≥8192 where RAM allows.

# Temperature (0.0-1.0): 0=deterministic, 1=creative
# 0.3 = grounded QA (live value); empty = Ollama default

# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Consumed by the Ollama SERVICE, not by this application
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The keys above are read by our Python. The keys below are read by the
# `ollama serve` PROCESS, and this application never looks them up. They
# belong in the SERVICE's environment — a shell before `ollama serve`, a
# systemd unit, or a container `environment:` block — NOT in this file.
# Setting them here does nothing to the engine: nothing in app/ reads them,
# and `.env` is not in the server's environment.
#
# They are listed anyway, for two reasons. An operator tuning this box will
# look for them here first. And `config_truth.sweep_inert_keys()` reports any
# `.env` key that no code path reads as INERT — so if one of these IS placed
# somewhere the sweep can see, the sweep would call a live engine setting
# dead. `_EXTERNALLY_CONSUMED` carves them out for that reason, as an exact
# list rather than an `OLLAMA` prefix: a prefix would silently excuse any
# future OLLAMA_* key that nothing reads, which is the one thing the sweep
# exists to catch.
#
# Values in [brackets] are what this box's `ollama serve` reports in its own
# startup line (`msg="server config"`) with nothing set; all are defaults.
# The one place in this repository that DOES set any of them is
# docker-compose.yml, for its `ollama` container (OLLAMA_HOST=0.0.0.0:11434
# and OLLAMA_MODELS) — which is this pattern done correctly: a container
# `environment:` block, not `.env`.
#
#   OLLAMA_NUM_PARALLEL                                    [1]
#       Generation slots. At 1 the second caller queues. Raising it to 2 was
#       tried here and REVERTED — it did not earn its ~1.5 GiB
#       (doc/perf/IMPLEMENTATION_STATUS.md). KV cache scales with
#       num_ctx x parallel, so raise it only against a measured queue term.
#
#   OLLAMA_MAX_LOADED_MODELS                               [0 = auto]
#       How many models stay resident. The embedder and the LLM compete for
#       the card; check `ollama ps` under load to confirm one has not evicted
#       the other before assuming residency.
#
#   OLLAMA_FLASH_ATTENTION                                 [false]
#   OLLAMA_KV_CACHE_TYPE                                   [empty = f16]
#       The pair that would shrink KV memory. Verify each key exists in the
#       INSTALLED Ollama version before relying on it — the names have moved.
#
#   OLLAMA_CONTEXT_LENGTH                                  [0 = model default]
#       A server-side ceiling on num_ctx. Ours is per-request (OLLAMA_NUM_CTX
#       above); a server ceiling set LOWER than that silently clamps it, and
#       the symptom is truncated answers, not an error.
#
#   OLLAMA_HOST                            [http://127.0.0.1:11434]
#       What the server BINDS. Our client's target is OLLAMA_URL above. The
#       two are independent settings that happen to agree here. docker-compose
#       sets this to 0.0.0.0:11434 so the container is reachable from the app
#       container — the same relationship, expressed in the service env.
#
#   OLLAMA_MODELS                              [~/.ollama/models]
#       Where model blobs live. docker-compose sets it per container. On a
#       host install, changing it relocates every model, so treat it as a
#       relocation rather than a tuning knob.
#
#   OLLAMA_KEEP_ALIVE                                      [5m0s]  ← THE TRAP
#       This name is consumed TWICE, by two different processes, and the two
#       are not the same setting:
#         * in THIS file it is OUR per-request value (see OLLAMA_KEEP_ALIVE
#           above — -1 = never unload), sent on every generation request;
#         * in the SERVER's environment it is the default the server applies
#           to requests that omit the field.
#       Setting it here does NOT change the server's default; setting it
#       there does not change what we send. The measured 32,919 ms cold load
#       came from exactly this confusion, which is why our client sends the
#       value on every request rather than relying on the server default.
#       It is deliberately NOT in `_EXTERNALLY_CONSUMED`: this application
#       really does read it, so it can never be inert.

# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Apple MLX LLM Server Configuration (macOS Apple Silicon)
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# mlx_lm.server (OpenAI-compatible) started by start_services.sh /
# bootstrap_services.py — replaces Ollama for LLM inference on Mac.

# MLX model repo on HuggingFace (~9 GB download, ~11 GB unified RAM).
# Smaller/faster alternative: mlx-community/Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct-4bit
# Sized by scripts/predeploy.py — see the Machine Profile block at the bottom.

# Local embedding model on Mac (no Ollama there); 768-dim nomic family,
# dimension-compatible with the Ollama nomic-embed-text store.
# Sized by scripts/predeploy.py — see the Machine Profile block at the bottom.

# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# FastAPI Server Configuration
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Port to run FastAPI on
FASTAPI_PORT=8000

# Host to bind to (0.0.0.0 = accept from any host)
FASTAPI_HOST=0.0.0.0

# Number of worker processes (in production)
# Sized by scripts/predeploy.py — see the Machine Profile block at the bottom.

# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Streamlit Web UI Configuration
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Port to run Streamlit on (usually FASTAPI_PORT + 501)
STREAMLIT_PORT=8501

# Run in headless mode (no browser opening)
STREAMLIT_SERVER_HEADLESS=true

# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Speech-to-Text (Whisper) Configuration
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Model size (options: tiny.en, base.en, small.en, medium.en, large)
# tiny: 39M, base: 74M, small: 244M, medium: 769M, large: 3.1G
# Sized by scripts/predeploy.py — see the Machine Profile block at the bottom.

# Number of threads for computation
# Sized by scripts/predeploy.py — see the Machine Profile block at the bottom.

# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Endpointing — the end-of-speech decision (BRD-04)
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# BRD-04 requires exactly ONE live end-of-speech decision, its delay
# documented, and its value reconciled against BRD-02. This is it. There is no
# second VAD on the Twilio path; a second Silero VAD with stop_secs=0.5 used to
# be constructed in app/pipeline.py and never attached to anything, announcing
# itself in the log as if it were live. It was removed.
#
# 600 ms is the pause tolerance given to the caller, and it is 86% of BRD-02's
# 700 ms budget before STT has run (AS-04), so BRD-02 is not reachable by
# tuning this number. Do not lower it casually: the load fixtures encode a
# 240 ms mid-turn hesitation as real caller behaviour, so anything at or below
# ~400 ms will split callers who pause mid-sentence -- and the harness CANNOT
# detect that, because its fixtures present no hesitation between 240 ms and
# the threshold. A latency win taken here is a win the test suite cannot audit.
VAD_SILENCE_MS=600

# Speculative STT: transcribe the audio-so-far while the remaining pause is
# still being counted, so the ~200 ms STT pass is no longer added to the
# caller's wait. This does NOT close the turn early and does NOT change the
# delay -- the caller still gets the full VAD_SILENCE_MS. The result is used
# only if the audio did not change (caller resumed -> discarded), so the
# transcript is identical by construction and the caller hears no difference.
# 0 disables it. 220 ms is the measured STT p50 (203 ms) rounded up.
VAD_SPECULATIVE_ADVANCE_MS=220

# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Text-to-Speech (Kokoro) Configuration
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Voice to use (options: af_heart, am_adam, bf_emma, bm_george, etc)
KOKORO_VOICE=af_heart

# Speaking speed (0.5-2.0, default: 1.0)
KOKORO_SPEED=1.0

# Streaming TTS (US-005, build-approved 2026-09-19; adoption gated on DG-03).
# 1 = the first sentence of an answer is synthesised and sent while the rest
# of the answer still awaits synthesis (measured: first-audio p50 2,250 ms vs
# 2,453 batch; over-cap 13% vs 27%). 0 = the batch path, retained unchanged
# as the BRD-15 revert -- this one line is the whole rollback.
TTS_STREAM=0

# Streaming LLM generation (US-004, build-approved 2026-09-19; adoption
# gated on DG-03). 1 = the answer is consumed as clauses and (with
# TTS_STREAM=1) synthesised while the model decodes (measured: first-audio
# p50 1,625 ms vs 2,453 batch; over-cap 6% vs 27%). KNOWN DEFECT: the
# streamed path froze the event loop after ~35 turns under N=2 (suspect:
# phonemization on the loop under concurrent streams) -- keep 0 until the
# root-cause fix lands. 0 = the batch path, the BRD-15 revert.
LLM_STREAM=0

# TTS cache scope (US-012 AC-4).
#   shared    one process-wide cache, reused by every caller (the DG-06 design)
#   per_call  bounded to one session's utterances; no cross-caller reuse
# Default is per_call because the N=2 isolation condition FAILED: 30
# occurrences of caller B being served audio synthesised during caller A's
# session (MOD-04 A.5.2). AC-4 pre-decides that outcome -- the cache becomes
# per-call rather than the finding being argued away. Reverting to shared is
# this one line; no code change is involved.
TTS_CACHE_SCOPE=per_call

# Two-caller admission control (US-016 / BRD-05).
# A third inbound call is refused at the voice endpoint, hears the
# pre-synthesised busy message and connects no media stream -- rather than all
# three degrading. The limit is the BRD-05 sizing for this box. Set
# ADMISSION_ENABLED=false to restore the previous behaviour in one step.
# After a KOKORO_VOICE / KOKORO_SPEED change, rebuild the call assets:
#   .venv/Scripts/python.exe scripts/build_call_assets.py
MAX_CONCURRENT_CALLS=2
ADMISSION_ENABLED=true

# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Caller interruption (US-014, Option 2 — "enable with bounded guard")
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Whether the caller can be heard while the agent is speaking.
#
# ON by default: the voice yields. A caller who speaks over the agent takes the
# floor — the agent stops mid-sentence, the carrier's buffered audio is flushed,
# and the interrupting words become the next turn.
#
# Setting this to 0 is the BRD-15 revert, and note what it reverts TO: the
# caller's overlapping speech is discarded and their partial utterance is reset.
# That is what the design documents always described and what the code did NOT
# do — before US-014 the guard was unreachable, so the real behaviour was to
# buffer the overlap and answer it as a fresh question after playback finished,
# i.e. the agent ignored the interruption and then answered it late.
BARGE_IN_ENABLED=1

# The echo gate: a frame is caller speech only if it clears
#     max(BARGE_IN_ABS_FLOOR, ref_env * ECHO_GAIN * MARGIN)
# where ref_env is the RMS of the audio the carrier is playing out right now.
#
# ECHO_GAIN is a DECLARED ASSUMPTION about the carrier's acoustic path (how much
# of our own voice comes back), not a local measurement -- US-002 TAC-9 puts the
# real path out of reach. MARGIN is how far above that echo the caller must be,
# and is ours to tune. They are separate keys so the assumption is visible in
# the tuning record instead of hidden inside one ratio.
#
# The defaults below are MEASURED, not decided by taste:
#   doc/perf/tools/measure_us014_guard.py   (the sweep)
#   doc/perf/us014-echo-guard-tuning.md     (the record, the rule, the limits)
# At these values: 0 false-admits on our own voice across echo gains -6..-24 dB,
# and a caller at the agent's own level is heard in every cell at ~160 ms. The
# known limit is a caller 12 dB quieter than the passage they talk over.
BARGE_IN_ECHO_GAIN=0.5
BARGE_IN_MARGIN=2.0

# Above is_silent's own 80, or the gate admits frames the VAD then discards as
# silence; below the quietest speech decile measured on the fixtures (~157), or
# a softly-spoken interruption is gated out.
BARGE_IN_ABS_FLOOR=150

# Consecutive admitting frames before a run is believed (6 x 20 ms = 120 ms).
BARGE_IN_MIN_FRAMES=6

# Per-tick decay of the playback reference once the carrier's buffer is drained.
BARGE_IN_REF_DECAY=0.95

# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Voice Agent Identity (app/voice_system_prompt.py)
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# How the voice agent introduces itself on calls. Used to fill the
# [COMPANY_NAME] / [AGENT_NAME] placeholders in the production voice
# system prompt. COMPANY_SHORT_NAME is for sentences where the full legal
# name is too long; ADMISSIONS_EMAIL/PHONE are the published contact details
# used by refusals, the prompt and localized messages.
COMPANY_NAME=Dr. Shyama Prasad Mukherjee University
COMPANY_SHORT_NAME=DSPMU
AGENT_NAME=Alex
ADMISSIONS_EMAIL=registrar@dspmuranchi.ac.in
ADMISSIONS_PHONE=+91 930 455 261
UNIVERSITY_WEBSITE=https://dspmuranchi.ac.in

# Programme catalogue: "dspmu" is the production catalogue built from
# content/dspmu/facts.json.
PROGRAM_PROFILE=dspmu

# Offer-letter identity. Leave DEFAULT_PAYMENT_LINK empty unless the
# university publishes a payment URL; an empty value is better than a link
# to the wrong institution.
UNIVERSITY_NAME=Dr. Shyama Prasad Mukherjee University
OFFER_EMAIL=registrar@dspmuranchi.ac.in
DEFAULT_PAYMENT_LINK=

